# Installed by the nvtop-pin package.
#
# Keeps apt from replacing a locally built nvtop with the one from a
# repository. Every repository candidate for the nvtop package is given a
# negative priority, so it is never chosen on its own and `apt upgrade` and
# `apt full-upgrade` leave the installed package where it is, whatever version
# numbers the archive grows later on. (Naming a version outright still
# overrides this, as it does any pin: `apt install nvtop=3.2.0-2` installs
# that version.)
#
# apt applies the FIRST stanza that matches a version, so order matters here.
#
# The first stanza covers the installed version. Without it the "release *"
# wildcard below would match the dpkg status file too (it is a pseudo-release,
# a=now), leaving nvtop with no candidate at all: `apt install nvtop` would
# then answer "has no installation candidate" instead of "already the newest
# version". 100 is apt's own priority for a version that exists only in the
# status file, and it is deliberately not higher: once the installed version
# outranks an allowed origin's own priority, apt keeps it unconditionally and
# the escape hatch described at the end of this file silently does nothing,
# wherever that origin's stanza is placed.
Package: nvtop
Pin: release a=now
Pin-Priority: 100

# Everything that comes from an actual repository.
Package: nvtop
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# Notes
# -----
# This blocks every repository, PPAs included. With nvtop installed, `apt
# install nvtop` reports it as already the newest version; with nvtop absent,
# there is nothing for apt to install at all. (If nvtop was removed but not
# purged, apt makes the leftover version the candidate and reports a download
# failure instead -- `apt purge nvtop` clears that up.)
#
# To let one source back through, add a stanza naming that origin ABOVE the
# "release *" one, since the first match wins:
#
#   Package: nvtop
#   Pin: release o=LP-PPA-flexiondotorg-nvtop
#   Pin-Priority: 500
#
# If the locally built nvtop ever ends up with an unsatisfiable dependency, for
# example after a library transition, `apt --fix-broken install` cannot repair
# it by pulling the archive version and will offer to remove nvtop instead.
# Purge this package first if that happens.
#
# This file is a conffile, so `apt-get remove nvtop-pin` leaves it in place.
# Use `apt-get purge nvtop-pin` to drop the pin.
